Creating backups is only part of a disaster recovery strategy. Organisations must regularly validate that backups can be restored successfully.

A backup validation programme should include:

* Scheduled restore testing
* Integrity verification
* Documentation updates
* Recovery timing measurements
* Periodic review

For example, an IT team restores a database into an isolated environment every quarter to confirm that backup files are complete and usable.

A real-life use case is organisations discovering backup configuration issues during testing rather than during an actual emergency.

Regular recovery testing increases confidence in disaster recovery capabilities.
